# Daily Focus configuration.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the repo root and uncomment what you want to change.
# `.env` is gitignored, because it will hold org names and logins. Every line here
# is a default, so a `.env` with nothing uncommented changes nothing.
#
# A variable set in the real environment always beats the same one in this file.
# Restart the server after editing.

# ---- the profile -----------------------------------------------------------

# Which half of your life this instance briefs: `work` or `personal`. Each profile
# runs as its own server, with its own store and its own morning agent. It picks
# which prompt `npm run init` links into the store, and some defaults: `personal`
# switches the Jira board off, and away detection too. Every setting below still
# overrides it.
# DAILY_FOCUS_PROFILE=work

# ---- the store -------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the store lives. The briefing agent must be pointed at the same directory.
# DAILY_FOCUS_DATA=~/.daily-focus

# ---- the server ------------------------------------------------------------

# DAILY_FOCUS_PORT=4321

# Loopback only by default, since this is personal data.
# DAILY_FOCUS_HOST=127.0.0.1

# ---- the working day -------------------------------------------------------

# Whether the agenda looks for free windows and counts focus time left. Needs a
# start and end to the day, which an evening at home doesn't have, so the personal
# profile defaults to off: the agenda shows events only, and the header shows the
# next event instead of focus time.
# DAILY_FOCUS_FREE_WINDOWS=on

# Local hours the working day starts and ends, used when the brief doesn't say.
# Ignored when free windows are off.
# The brief's own dayStart / dayEnd win, since the agent read today's calendar.
# DAILY_FOCUS_WORK_START=9
# DAILY_FOCUS_WORK_END=17

# Minutes before a gap between meetings counts as a focus window.
# DAILY_FOCUS_MIN_FREE_WINDOW=45

# ---- the morning agent -----------------------------------------------------

# Hours before a refresh is expected, then a 45-minute grace period before warning.
# Counted only in hours a run was due, so a weekend never trips it.
# DAILY_FOCUS_STALE_AFTER_HOURS=24

# Weekdays the agent is scheduled on, cron-numbered: 1-5 is Monday to Friday,
# 0-4 is Sunday to Thursday. Inferred from the archive when unset.
# DAILY_FOCUS_AGENT_DAYS=1-5

# ---- the focus timer -------------------------------------------------------

# Default length of a focus session, in minutes.
# DAILY_FOCUS_SESSION_MINUTES=25

# Minutes of an untouched machine before a running session is closed back at the
# last sign of life. 0 turns it off. Defaults to 0 for the personal profile.
# DAILY_FOCUS_AWAY_AFTER=10

# ---- the pull request board ------------------------------------------------

# The board polls GitHub for the pull requests you have open, using whatever
# account the gh CLI is logged in as. Set to `off` to disable it entirely.
# DAILY_FOCUS_GITHUB=on

# Logins to poll as, comma-separated, when gh holds more than one account or the
# active one isn't the one you want. Each is resolved with `gh auth token --user`.
# Unset means the account gh has active.
# DAILY_FOCUS_GITHUB_ACCOUNTS=alice,alice_corp

# Only count pull requests in these organisations or repositories, comma-separated.
# An entry with a slash is a repository, anything else an organisation. Unset means
# every pull request the account has open anywhere.
# DAILY_FOCUS_GITHUB_SCOPE=acme,acme-labs/webapp

# Status checks that stand for a repository's whole merge policy, comma-separated
# and spelled exactly as GitHub spells them — commas only, since check names
# contain spaces. When one of these hasn't finished, the pull request gets its own
# "Waiting on merge gate" bucket instead of being called ready, because such a
# check refusing says the merge is blocked without saying whose move it is.
# Unset means no check gets that treatment. The names below are invented.
# DAILY_FOCUS_GITHUB_MERGE_GATE_CHECKS=policy/merge-gate,repository-policy

# Minutes between polls while a browser tab is open on the dashboard.
# DAILY_FOCUS_GITHUB_POLL_MINUTES=5

# Path to the gh binary, for when the server's PATH doesn't include it.
# DAILY_FOCUS_GH=/opt/homebrew/bin/gh

# ---- the live agenda (macOS only) ------------------------------------------

# The agenda on the right is read from Calendar.app, so a meeting you decline at
# eleven frees the slot without waiting for tomorrow's brief. Needs
# `npm run build:calendar` once, and a one-time calendar permission prompt.
# Set to `off` to fall back to the events in the brief.
# DAILY_FOCUS_CALENDAR=on

# Which calendars to show, comma-separated — commas only, since calendar names
# contain spaces. Spell them as Calendar.app spells them, which is not always how
# your agent's sources.md spells them. Unset means nothing is read and the brief's
# events are used. A name matching nothing is reported rather than ignored.
# DAILY_FOCUS_CALENDARS=Work,Personal

# Your own addresses, comma-separated. Used only to find your own reply among an
# event's attendees, so a meeting you declined stops eating your focus time.
# Without this, declined meetings still show and still block.
# DAILY_FOCUS_CALENDAR_ADDRESSES=you@example.com,you@personal.example

# Minutes between calendar reads while a browser tab is open.
# DAILY_FOCUS_CALENDAR_POLL_MINUTES=5

# Path to the helper bundle, if it isn't the one npm run build:calendar makes.
# DAILY_FOCUS_CALENDAR_APP=~/Applications/Daily Focus Calendar.app

# ---- the Jira ticket board -------------------------------------------------

# The third tab flags tickets whose status doesn't match their pull requests:
# nothing open left but not Done, work started without the ticket moving, or a
# ticket claiming to be in flight with no code linked at all. It borrows the
# Atlassian CLI's session, so the setup is `acli jira auth login` and nothing
# else. Set to `off` to disable it entirely. Defaults to off for the personal
# profile.
# DAILY_FOCUS_JIRA=on

# Only look at these projects, comma-separated. Unset means every project your
# account can see, which is usually right — the search is already limited to
# tickets assigned to you, or raised by you and still unassigned.
# DAILY_FOCUS_JIRA_PROJECTS=PROJ,OTHER

# Statuses in which standing still is deliberate rather than an oversight,
# comma-separated and spelled as your Jira spells them — commas only, since
# status names contain spaces. A ticket in one of these is already saying why it
# isn't moving, so it is not also flagged for having no code linked to it. It is
# still flagged when all of its pull requests have closed, because that leaves
# the question of what it is waiting for. Unset means no status is exempt.
# DAILY_FOCUS_JIRA_HOLD_STATUSES=Blocked,On Hold

# The statuses the tab's "Working on" view shows, comma-separated and spelled
# as your Jira spells them. Jira counts In Review as in progress too, so this is
# how you narrow the view to the work in your own hands. Case doesn't matter, so
# one name covers projects that capitalise it differently. Unset shows every
# ticket Jira has in progress, including ones that are also out of sync.
# DAILY_FOCUS_JIRA_IN_PROGRESS_STATUSES=In Progress

# Minutes between reads while a browser tab is open. Slower than the pull
# request board's by default: a mislabelled ticket is never urgent.
# DAILY_FOCUS_JIRA_POLL_MINUTES=15

# Your Atlassian site, used to build the browse links. Unset means whichever
# site acli is authenticated to, which is almost always what you want; set it
# only if your browse host differs. A pasted URL is fine.
# DAILY_FOCUS_JIRA_SITE=acme.atlassian.net

# Path to the acli binary, for when the server's PATH doesn't include it.
# DAILY_FOCUS_ACLI=/opt/homebrew/bin/acli

# ---- the assistant ---------------------------------------------------------

# An assistant in every item's panel that runs a coding-agent CLI you already have,
# headless, against that one item: is this review right, why is CI failing,
# draft a reply to this thread. `claude` or `codex`; `off` hides the button.
# The assistant reads and drafts. It never sends, posts, or edits a file, and
# it gets no checkout: fixing conflicts or CI is work for a real coding session.
# DAILY_FOCUS_ASSISTANT=off

# Path to the CLI, for when the server's PATH doesn't include it.
# DAILY_FOCUS_ASSISTANT_BIN=/opt/homebrew/bin/claude

# Model and effort, passed to the CLI as they are. Unset means no flag, so the
# CLI uses whatever you configured in it. Claude Code takes an alias like `opus`
# or a full id; Codex takes its own model names. Effort is low, medium, high,
# xhigh or max on both.
# DAILY_FOCUS_ASSISTANT_MODEL=opus
# DAILY_FOCUS_ASSISTANT_EFFORT=high

# What Claude Code may use without asking, in its own permission syntax and
# comma-separated, since a rule has a space in it. Everything else is denied,
# and the editing tools are denied whatever this says. The default is the two
# CLIs the boards use, fetching a page, and the claude.ai Gmail connector, which
# is how a draft lands in the thread. Codex ignores this: its sandbox, confined
# to the empty working directory, is the policy.
# DAILY_FOCUS_ASSISTANT_TOOLS=Bash(gh *),Bash(acli *),WebFetch,mcp__claude_ai_Gmail

# ---- rerunning the morning agent -------------------------------------------

# A refresh icon beside the brief's age that runs the morning agent now, through
# a CLI you already have: for the day that has moved on from the morning's brief.
# `codex` or `claude`; `off` hides it. It uses the same wrapper prompt as your
# scheduled task, in the store, and writes the brief exactly as that does. The
# scheduler stays where the agent normally runs.
# DAILY_FOCUS_AGENT=off

# Path to the CLI, for when the server's PATH doesn't include it.
# DAILY_FOCUS_AGENT_BIN=/opt/homebrew/bin/codex

# Model and effort, passed to the CLI as they are. Set them to what your
# scheduled task uses, so a brief started here is as thorough as a scheduled
# one. Unset means no flag, and the CLI's own defaults.
# DAILY_FOCUS_AGENT_MODEL=
# DAILY_FOCUS_AGENT_EFFORT=xhigh

# What Claude Code may use to reach your sources, in its own permission syntax
# and comma-separated. Writing items.json, and nothing else in the store, is
# allowed whatever this says. The default covers the two CLIs, fetching a page,
# and the claude.ai Gmail connector; add your calendar, chat and document
# connectors by name. Codex ignores this: its sandbox, confined to the store, is
# the policy.
# DAILY_FOCUS_AGENT_TOOLS=Bash(gh *),Bash(acli *),WebFetch,mcp__claude_ai_Gmail
